@page "/docs/common/accessibility"

@* Accessibility across the library: native elements, names, keyboard and focus, announcements,
   color, motion, JavaScript off, and what the app still owns. Checked against the source
   (base.css focus and forced-colors, focus.ts, Icon.cs, Image.cs, Alert/Banner/ToastRegion,
   AppShell's skip link). Each component's Overview has its own Accessibility section. *@

<ArticlePage Section="common" Slug="accessibility">
    <Lead>
        IzzyBlazor builds on the browser's own elements, so keyboard, focus and screen readers work as the platform defines
        them, and the script adds ARIA only where it adds behavior. This page covers what the components do for you, and
        what's left to your app.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Native elements first</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Buttons are <Code>button</Code> or <Code>a</Code>, dialogs are <Code>dialog</Code>, selects are
            <Code>select</Code>, disclosures are <Code>details</Code>, and checkboxes, radios and switches are real inputs.
            Enter, Space, Escape and the arrow keys do what people expect, with no script.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Where the script adds behavior, it adds the roles that go with it. Tabs are a radio group with JavaScript off and
            become ARIA tabs with roving focus once the script runs; a dialog opened by the server becomes a real modal. Each
            component's Overview says what changes in its Mode support table.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Names and labels</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>Fields render a <Code>label</Code> linked to the control, and link their hint and errors with <Code>aria-describedby</Code>. <Code>HideLabel</Code> keeps the label for screen readers when it isn't shown.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Anything shown only as an icon needs a name: <Anchor Href="docs/components/icon-button">IconButton</Anchor>'s <Code>Label</Code> is required.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>An <Anchor Href="docs/components/icon">Icon</Anchor> is hidden from screen readers unless you give it a <Code>Label</Code>, so it never repeats the text beside it.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/image">Image</Anchor> requires <Code>Alt</Code>; pass <Code>Alt=""</Code> for a picture that only decorates.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Components that hold a region, such as a table, a menu or pagination, take a <Code>Label</Code> or <Code>Caption</Code> for their name, with a default from <Anchor Href="docs/common/text">IzzyText</Anchor>.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Keyboard and focus</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>The focus ring shows for keyboard focus only, in the focus color (<Code>--ib-color-focus</Code>, your brand color by default).</ListItem>
            <ListItem>After a failed submit, <Code>FormErrors</Code> takes focus, with JavaScript off too, so people start from the list of problems.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Dialogs and drawers trap focus and return it to the trigger when they close; a popover opened by the user takes focus and gives it back.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/app-shell">AppShell</Anchor> starts with a "Skip to content" link to the main area.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>When Blazor's <Code>FocusOnNavigate</Code> moves focus to the page's heading, or the skip link to the main area, no ring is drawn there: screen readers still announce it, and sighted users don't see a box they didn't ask for.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Announcements</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>Danger and warning alerts are announced as soon as they appear; the others politely. A <Code>Banner</Code> isn't announced, since it's there when the page loads; set <Code>Announce</Code> when one appears later.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Toasts are read politely, and stay until dismissed when they're errors.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>A loading button sets <Code>aria-busy</Code> and is heard as its <Code>LoadingLabel</Code>; a <Code>Spinner</Code> is heard as its <Code>Label</Code>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>A table's "1–10 of 50" is read when it changes.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>A status, such as a badge's or an alert's, comes with an icon and words as well as a color, and screen readers hear its name.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Color and contrast</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Text holds 4.5:1 on what it sits on, in light and dark (disabled text, as WCAG allows, is lighter), and a brand or gray color of your own is checked the same
            way. In Windows high-contrast mode, controls switch to the system's colors, and checkboxes and switches fall back
            to the browser's own. When you set colors yourself, see
            <Anchor Href="docs/theming/tokens">Tokens and customizing</Anchor> for the pairs to keep together.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Motion</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            With reduced motion set, dialogs, drawers, popovers, tooltips, disclosures, tabs and the sidebar stop moving, and
            spinners slow down
            rather than stop, since the motion says "still working". There's no page-wide reset, so your own animations are
            yours to handle.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">With JavaScript off</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Everything stays usable, but a few things are plainer: tabs are heard as radio buttons, a dialog opened by the
            server isn't modal, and read-only checkboxes and radios are disabled, so they can't be focused. Each component's
            Overview lists what changes; see <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">Render modes</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">What's left to your app</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>A <Code>lang</Code> on the <Code>html</Code> element, and a <Code>PageTitle</Code> on every page.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>One <Code>h1</Code> per page and headings in order; <Code>Heading</Code>'s <Code>Size</Code> changes the look, not the level.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Link and button text that makes sense on its own, not "Click here".</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Alt text that says what a picture shows, and a unit in a field's label when it matters, as in "Price (USD)".</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Contrast for colors you set yourself, and testing with a keyboard and a screen reader.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/forms">Forms and validation</Anchor>: labels, hints and errors.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components">All components</Anchor>: what each one does in each mode.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>
